why: the operator asked for "нормальную понятную документацию" and I
  have delivered correctness instead of readability three times now.
  docs/benchmarks.md is 2 800 lines in which the answer to "is this
  library fast, and at what" is not findable: headline numbers sit
  beside the archaeology of every refuted experiment, and a day of
  corrections has layered "this used to say X" on top.

what: a way IN, not a rewrite. The history of refuted attempts is the
  most valuable thing on the page — the `performance` skill exists
  because that history stops work being redone — so nothing is
  deleted. What is missing is a first screen:

  1. ONE table of the headline numbers, a row per question, with the
     section that explains each.
  2. A short "how to read this page" that says what the sections are
     for and where the corrections live, so a reader knows the long
     prose is a record and not a preamble.
  3. The Lane rules promoted out of the tail, since they are what a
     contributor needs BEFORE adding a lane, not after.

predict: nothing measurable. This is a documentation change and its
  test is whether someone can answer "how fast is the JSON reader"
  without scrolling. No numbers move; if any number in the new summary
  disagrees with its section, the summary is wrong, not the section.
